<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_potential-app" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Applications of Electric Potential</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in an interactive studio classroom, after students have completed <xref ref="sec_reps-potential"/> and its prerequisite sections.  The activities take about 90 minutes.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

<subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
  </subsubsection>

<activity>
	<title>Equipotential Surfaces</title>
	<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		This activity uses a plastic surface, like the one in the figure, to represent the electric potential between two charged plates, one positive and one negative.  A <m>1 \mathrm{~cm}</m> height difference corresponds to an electric potential difference of <m>1 \mathrm{~V}</m>.
	</p>
	<ol marker="A.">
		<li>
			<p>
				Rank the three marked points by the value of the electric potential from greatest to least.  Explain your reasoning.
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				Find three different points on the surface that are separated by equal (non-zero!) changes in potential.
			</p>
						  <ol marker="1.">
							<li>
							  <p>
								Identify (and mark) all the points with the same value of potential as your three points.
							  </p>
							</li>
			
							<li>
							  <p>
								What patterns do you notice?
							  </p>
							</li>
						  </ol>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				Align your surface with a contour map.  How are you making this alignment?  Where is the surface a good approximation of the potential?  Where is the approximation less good?
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				Sketch a graph of the potential <m>V</m> vs. distance from the negative plate <m>x</m>.
			</p>
			<p>
						  <ol marker="1.">
							<li>
							  <p>
								Describe the relationship between potential and distance from the negative plate.
							  </p>
							</li>
			
							<li>
							  <p>
								Propose an equation to describe the electric potential as a function of the distance, <m>x</m>, from the negative plate. Where did choose for the location of <m>V = 0</m>?
							  </p>
							</li>
			
							<li>
							  <p>
								What do you think the slope of your graph of <m>V</m> vs. <m>x</m> represents?
							  </p>
							</li>
						  </ol>
						</p>
		</li>
	</ol>
</activity>

